    "## Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Which combination of browsing-related features (e.g., product page engagement, informational content depth, media value) and session context variables (e.g., month, weekend, special day proximity, traffic source, visitor type) is associated with the highest probability of generating revenue, and how do these optimal feature combinations vary across different shopping occasions?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Online shopping websites usually have a large number of visitors but only a small percentage of them actually make a purchase. That pattern is not surprising from a consumer perspective. Most people browse, compare options, and leave without buying. Because of this, researchers focus on identifying signals during a browsing session that indicate stronger purchase intent and understanding the conditions that increase the likelihood of conversion. Rather than treating a purchase as a single isolated event, many studies describe it as a process that includes searching, comparing alternatives, and evaluating information. For example, when buying a new pair of running shoes, a customer will likely open multiple tabs, compare prices and reviews, and revisit certain pages before deciding. This is why session-level features such as time spent on product pages, number of pages viewed, and types of content accessed are commonly used to predict purchases.<a name=\"cite_ref-1\"></a>[<sup>1</sup>](#cite_note-1)\n",
    "\n",
    "Research shows that browsing behavior within a single session can be highly informative for predicting whether a user will buy something. Studies published in *Decision Support Systems* demonstrate that combining browsing data with customer characteristics, such as whether a visitor is new or returning, improves prediction accuracy.<a name=\"cite_ref-2\"></a>[<sup>2</sup>](#cite_note-2) Similarly, research in *Electronic Markets* finds that clickstream data, including navigation depth and page sequence patterns, can effectively predict purchase intention.<a name=\"cite_ref-3\"></a>[<sup>3</sup>](#cite_note-3) These findings support the idea that engagement metrics alone are useful, but they are more powerful when analyzed alongside contextual variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "More recent work suggests that the order and structure of browsing also matter. The way users move through a website can indicate whether they are casually exploring or shopping with a specific goal in mind. For example, directly searching for a product using the search bar may reflect clearer intent than navigating through the homepage and category pages. These browsing patterns can shift depending on promotions or specific shopping occasions, which implies that the most predictive engagement features may vary across contexts.<a name=\"cite_ref-4\"></a>[<sup>4</sup>](#cite_note-4)\n",
    "\n",
    "Time-related factors also play a meaningful role in purchasing behavior. Reports from organizations such as the National Retail Federation and Adobe show that online spending increases substantially during holidays and major shopping events.<a name=\"cite_ref-5\"></a>[<sup>5</sup>](#cite_note-5) <a name=\"cite_ref-6\"></a>[<sup>6</sup>](#cite_note-6) This supports including variables such as month and proximity to special days when analyzing revenue outcomes. Retailers often track important dates, such as birthdays or seasonal events, and offer targeted promotions during those times to encourage purchases.\n",
    "\n",
    "Traffic source and visitor type are also consistently linked to conversion differences. Direct traffic and returning visitors often have higher purchase rates, likely because they are already familiar with the brand or have stronger purchase intent. Google Analytics formally defines traffic channel categories (e.g., Direct, Organic Search, Referral), reinforcing the idea that acquisition source should be treated as a key predictor of conversion.<a name=\"cite_ref-7\"></a>[<sup>7</sup>](#cite_note-7)\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### References\n",
    "\n",
    "1. <a name=\"cite_note-1\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-1) Tan, Y. (2025). *Understanding consumers’ online browsing patterns and their relationship with purchase behavior in e-commerce.*  \n",
    "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11747-025-01106-4\n",
    "\n",
    "2. <a name=\"cite_note-2\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-2) *Predicting online customer purchase: The integration of customer characteristics and website browsing behavior.* Decision Support Systems.  \n",
    "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S016792362300180X\n",
    "\n",
    "3. <a name=\"cite_note-3\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-3) *Customer purchase prediction in electronic markets from clickstream data.* Electronic Markets.  \n",
    "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12351-023-00813-6\n",
    "\n",
    "4. <a name=\"cite_note-4\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-4) Tan, Y. (2025). *Understanding consumers’ online browsing patterns…*  \n",
    "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11747-025-01106-4\n",
    "\n",
    "5. <a name=\"cite_note-5\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-5) National Retail Federation (NRF). *Winter Holiday Data and Trends.*  \n",
    "https://nrf.com/research-insights/holiday-data-and-trends/winter-holidays\n",
    "\n",
    "6. <a name=\"cite_note-6\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-6) Adobe Newsroom. *Holiday Shopping Season Report.*  \n",
    "https://news.adobe.com/news/2026/01/adobe-holiday-shopping-season\n",
    "\n",
    "7. <a name=\"cite_note-7\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-7) Google Analytics Help. *Default Channel Group Definitions.*  \n",
    "https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/9756891?hl=en\n",
    "\n"
   ]
