   "source": [
    "## Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Revenue is most likely to happen when someone is genuinely interested in a product and the timing is right. If a user is spending real time on product pages, looking through details, and watching media, and on top of that they're a returning visitor, shopping on a weekend, close to a big sale, or coming from a source that shows they already have some intent, they're a lot more likely to actually buy something.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Sub-Hypotheses\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Engagement Effect: Users who actually look around and spend time on product pages should be more likely to convert than users who just bounce. The more someone engages, the more likely they are to buy.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Contextual Amplification: Engagement probably matters even more depending on when someone visits. Peak shopping months, weekends, and days around big promotions should push engaged users even closer to making a purchase.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Visitor Type Interaction: Returning visitors who are also highly engaged are probably the most likely to convert out of any group. They've been to the site before and came back, so pairing that with strong engagement should be a pretty reliable indicator of purchase intent.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Traffic Source Differentiation: The source someone comes from likely says a lot about their intent. Direct traffic probably signals stronger purchase intent than something like a random referral, so the same engagement behavior might lead to different conversion rates depending on where the user came from.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. Occasion-Specific Variation: The features that best predict a purchase probably aren't the same across all time periods. What works during the holiday season likely looks pretty different from what drives conversions in the middle of summer, meaning the best predictors are probably context-dependent rather than universal.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
